s=s[:a]+r'''<h3 id="s42">4.2. Spatial formulations and the continuous reference</h3>
<p>The error decomposition in (17) suggests two complementary comparisons. Evaluating the exact finite-lattice fields against the continuous solution measures the profile difference introduced by the lattice. Evolving all three spatial formulations from continuous initial fields then shows how their errors develop in computation. We use the same physical-coordinate and cell-average references in both comparisons, so that the measured differences retain the interpretation established above.</p>
<p>We first evaluate the exact finite-lattice solutions at \\(T=0.25\\). Table 1 reports their continuous-reference errors for three reciprocal spacings. The observed order is computed as \\(\log_2(E(a)/E(a/2))\\). Since this calculation uses exact formulas, its refinement trend measures the spatial approximation independently of a time integrator.</p>
{{TABLE_MODEL}}
<p>Both fields approach their continuous references as the lattice is refined. The two-soliton profile has larger errors at the same spacing, showing that the size of the profile discrepancy also depends on the wave being represented. These values provide a reference for interpreting the evolved computations below.</p>
<p>For the numerical evolutions, all three spatial methods start from the continuous initial fields and use RK4 with \\(\Delta T=5\times10^{-4}\\). Tables 2 and 3 report terminal errors at \\(T=0.25\\); Figures 3 and 4 show field profiles and spatial errors at the midpoint \\(T=0.125\\). The reciprocal spacing \\(a\\) and fixed physical spacing \\(\Delta x\\) take the same nominal values, giving the same node count at each resolution. Their physical locations follow the respective coordinate definitions. The time-step-halving check at spacing 0.02 changes the evolved states by less than \\(3\times10^{-14}\\), placing the reported spatial errors well above that observed temporal refinement difference.</p>
{{FIG_SPATIAL}}
<p>The two cases show the same overall pattern: in the reported field metrics, the direct FD formulations produce smaller errors than integrable SD, and their advantage increases under spatial refinement. The observed orders explain this trend. Integrable SD gives approximately first-order error reduction, while the direct FD formulations give approximately second-order reduction. Halving the spacing therefore reduces the SD errors by about two and the direct FD errors by about four. The coefficients and wave profile determine the absolute magnitudes within this refinement pattern.</p>
<p>This distinction can be traced to the analytical relations. The lattice phase expansion following (7) has a leading correction proportional to \\(a\\), consistent with the exact-profile errors in Table 1. Equation (10) identifies the density-dependent term \\(ac(1-\rho_k^2)\\) in the difference between the two moving-grid slope equations. It is positive in the soliton density depressions, increasing the SD slope rate relative to moving FD at the same state. Because the velocity is reconstructed by accumulating jumps from the left boundary, this local difference can contribute to a discrepancy farther along the chain, consistent with the downstream velocity errors in both figures. The density responds through the changing cell widths and consequently has a different error distribution.</p>
<p>The comparison thus connects the numerical observations with a specific feature of the spatial model. The exact lattice family identifies the approximation order before time stepping, and the evolved cases show how the resulting differences appear in the two physical fields. Together they explain why the first-order integrable formulation is less accurate than the second-order direct formulations in these smooth-wave tests.</p>
'''.replace('\\\\','\\')+s[b:]
p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
